@@ -0,0 +1,100 @@
+# Partial Structuring
+
+```{versionadded} 25.4.0
+
+```
+
+Sometimes the data you want to structure is incomplete or partially invalid, and
+you'd like to salvage as much of it as possible instead of raising an error.
+
+_cattrs_ offers partial structuring for this purpose, via the
+{meth}`BaseConverter.partial_structure <cattrs.BaseConverter.partial_structure>`
+method (also available as the top-level {func}`cattrs.partial_structure`).
+
+Instead of raising, partial structuring returns a
+{class}`PartialResult <cattrs.PartialResult>` containing:
+
+- `value`: the best-effort structured value, or `None` if it can't be created;
+- `is_complete`: whether the structuring was fully successful;
+- `structured_fields`: the names of the fields structured successfully from the input;
+- `failed_fields`: the names of the fields that failed to structure (including fields
+  absent from the input);
+- `errors`: an exception summarizing the failures, or `None`;
+- `error_map`: a mapping of field names to the exceptions raised for those fields.
+
+```python
+>>> from attrs import define
+>>> from cattrs import Converter
+>>> @define
+... class Class:
+...     an_int: int
+...     a_string: str = "default"
+>>> c = Converter()
+>>> res = c.partial_structure({}, Class)
+>>> res.is_complete
+False
+>>> res.value  # The int has no default, so no value can be created.
+None
+>>> res.failed_fields
+frozenset({'an_int', 'a_string'})
+```
+
+Failed fields with defaults use those defaults as fallback:
+
+```python
+>>> res = c.partial_structure({"an_int": 1}, Class)
+>>> res.value
+Class(an_int=1, a_string='default')
+>>> res.structured_fields
+frozenset({'an_int'})
+>>> res.failed_fields
+frozenset({'a_string'})
+```
+
+## Nested Classes
+
+Fields of _attrs_ classes, dataclasses and TypedDicts are partially structured
+recursively. If the nested object is only partially complete, its partial value
+is used, and the parent field is marked as failed:
+
+```python
+>>> @define
+... class Inner:
+...     x: int
+...     y: int = 0
+>>> @define
+... class Outer:
+...     inner: Inner
+>>> res = c.partial_structure({"inner": {"x": 1}}, Outer)
+>>> res.value
+Outer(inner=Inner(x=1, y=0))
+>>> res.failed_fields
+frozenset({'inner'})
+```
+
+Collection fields (`list`, `dict`, ...) are structured atomically: if any
+element fails to structure, the whole field is marked as failed.
+
+## Refining
+
+The {meth}`PartialResult.refine <cattrs.PartialResult.refine>` method can be
+used to fix the failed fields of a result with new data, while preserving the
+fields that were already structured successfully:
+
+```python
+>>> res = c.partial_structure({"an_int": 1}, Class)
+>>> res = res.refine({"a_string": "a string"})
+>>> res.is_complete
+True
+>>> res.value
+Class(an_int=1, a_string='a string')
+```
+
+## Notes
+
+- Fields with `init=False` are excluded from both `structured_fields` and
+  `failed_fields`.
+- With {class}`cattrs.Converter` (`forbid_extra_keys=True`), extra keys in
+  the input make `is_complete` `False`, but a value is still produced.
+- The `detailed_validation` parameter is respected and defaults to the
+  converter's setting.
